  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nSummer continues to keeps its hold on our region. An upper level \nridge over the southeastern U.S. and a return of southerly winds \nwill push high temperatures today into the 92 to 99 degree range, \nand the 94 to 101 degree range Wednesday.  Heat indices today will \nmax out around 100 degrees, and Wednesday in the 100 to 110 degree \nrange. If the model blend continues to produce these values, a \nHeat Advisory will likely be needed Wednesday for a portion of \nthe PAH forecast area (fa). This will also be dependent on \ntiming of potential clouds/precipitation Wednesday afternoon, \nwhich could tamp down these values, especially north.\n\nAs the upper level ridge weakens and moves eastward, models bring a \nslow moving surface cold front south toward our area late Wednesday. \nThe front will slowly makes its way across the PAH fa Thursday into \nThursday night.  Along and ahead of the front, chances of showers \nand thunderstorms will increase and spread from northwest to \nsoutheast late Wednesday into Wednesday night. The greatest \nchances (75-95%) will be late Wednesday night through Thursday \nmorning across southwest Indiana, southern Illinois and \nsoutheast Missouri, and through the day Thursday across west \nKentucky and extreme southeast Missouri. Good chances (40-60%) \nwill linger into Thursday night across mainly west Kentucky and\nportions of southeast Missouri. \n\nSPC Day 3 includes southern Illinois, southwest Indiana and\nportions of southeast Missouri in a Marginal Risk, with the main\nconcern with storms being gusty winds late Wednesday into\nWednesday night.\n\nThe cold front will hang up to our south as a weak upper level \ntrof lingers over the region. This will keep things unsettled \nFriday and the weekend, with on and off 20-40% chances of \nshowers and storms. \n\nThe passage of the cold front will bring temperatures down to\nmore seasonable readings Thursday and Friday. A return of\nsoutherly winds over the weekend will push high temperatures \nback up to around 90 degrees. Readings will then trend back down\nto more seasonable readings early next week.",
